.TH RUNTESTS 1
.SH NAME
runtests \- run the tests and say which failed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runtests
[
.B -g
]
[
.B -l
]
[
.B -v
]
[
.B -r
.I resultfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Runtests
runs the tests that need no screen and reports each one, then names the ones it
did not run.
.B -g
runs the ones that do need a screen instead, and must itself be started under
.IR wm (1).
.B -l
says what it would run and runs nothing.
.B -v
lets each test's own output through instead of keeping it.
.PP
There was no way to do this before.
The tests existed, they were listed in
.BR doc/hpc-plan.md ,
and each had a page saying how to run it \(em but running them meant running
each by hand and remembering which need a flag before they check anything at
all rather than merely reporting.
.SH "WHY IT IS A PROGRAM AND NOT A SCRIPT"
Each test is loaded and run in this process, and its exceptions are caught.
One test failing must not stop the rest from being tried, which is exactly what
a shell loop would do at the first non-zero status.
.PP
A test's own output is kept rather than discarded, because when one does fail
its last words are the only detail there is: Limbo gives the variable in an
exception clause a type of its own, so what was actually raised cannot be
turned into a string here.
The verdict line carries the last thing the test said.
.SS "The graphical ones"
None of them is given
.BR -q .
That flag ends the session, which would take this program with it before it
could report anything at all.
.PP
Four are still not run by anything here and are named rather than quietly
omitted.
.IR Clicktest (1)
has to be
.I driven
\(em something must click on it \(em which is a different shape from starting a
test and waiting;
.IR drawdecodetest (1)
wants a clip to hand;
.IR gputest (1)
is slow and wants a GPU; and
.IR fdstresstest (1)
hangs on failure, so it needs a timeout around it that this cannot provide.
.SH "THE FILE IS THE VERDICT\"
.B Emu
does not carry a Dis program's exit status out to whatever started it: a run
with a failing test exits 0 exactly like a clean one.
Anything deciding automatically has to read
.I resultfile
.RB ( /runtests.result
by default), which ends in
.B PASS
or
.BR FAIL .
.PP
This is the same caveat as
.IR wmtest (1)
and
.IR clicktest (1)
and for the same reason.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
% runtests
sessiontest          ok
styxtest             ok
refstress            ok

3 of 3 ran clean.
.EE
.PP
Under
.IR wm (1),
with
.BR -g :
.IP
.EX
nowmtest             ok
line3test            ok
keyuptest            ok
wmtest -n 40         ok
wmtest -k            ok
.EE
.PP
The arguments are part of the name, because the same test with different flags
is a different check and a listing saying
.B wmtest
twice says nothing about which of them failed.
.PP
With a fault put in on purpose, to show it can say so:
.IP
.EX
sessiontest          FAIL reported failure: sessiontest: 2 checks failed
wmtest -k            FAIL reported failure: wmtest: 4 labels drawn and read back, 1 failed
.EE
.PP
In both cases the other tests still ran, which is the point.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/runtests.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sessiontest (1),
.IR styxtest (1),
.IR refstress (1),
.IR wmtest (1),
.IR clicktest (1),
.IR faultprobe (1)
.PP
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
has the full table, including what each graphical test checks.
.SH BUGS
It runs three tests without
.BR -g ,
and five with it.
The numerics tests are in neither: they were not written to be run from inside
another program.
Naming a test is not the same as running it.
.PP
A test that spawns children may still let their output past, since only this
process's own descriptors are redirected.
.PP
Nothing here has a timeout.
A test that hangs hangs the run, and
.IR fdstresstest (1)
is documented to do exactly that on failure, which is one of the reasons it is
not in the list.
